<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · region</p><h1 class="enc-title">Maluku Islands</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Moluccas</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Maluku Islands, or Moluccas, are an archipelago in eastern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/indonesia">Indonesia</a>, historically known as the Spice Islands. They were the original source of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cloves">cloves</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nutmeg">nutmeg</a> and mace, which were highly prized in global trade. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dutch-east-india-company">Dutch East India Company</a> seized control of the spice trade there in the seventeenth century, making it central to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dutch-empire">Dutch Empire</a>'s economy.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/maluku-islands.jpg" alt="Maluku Islands" width="320" height="353" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Lencer · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Maluku_Islands_en.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>The Maluku Islands cover about 850,000 km2, 90 per cent of it sea, and number an estimated 1027 islands. The two largest, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/halmahera">Halmahera</a> and Seram, are sparsely populated, while the most developed, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ambon">Ambon</a> and Ternate, are small. Most of the islands are forested and mountainous, and several, such as Ternate, are volcanoes rising from the sea; there have been over 70 serious eruptions in the last 500 years. All but the Aru group lie in Wallacea, between Weber's Line and Lydekker's Line, so their fauna is more Australasian than Asian.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>Australo-Melanesians were the first people on the islands, at least 40,000 years ago, and Austronesian speakers arrived around 2000 BCE. Arab merchants began to arrive in the fourteenth century, bringing <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/islam">Islam</a>, which took hold especially in the trading centres. The earliest recorded word identifiable with Maluku, Maloko, appears in the Old Javanese Nagarakertagama of 1365.</p><p>The Portuguese conquered <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/malacca">Malacca</a> in August 1511 and soon sent ships to the Spice Islands. Francisco Serrão, shipwrecked at Hitu on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ambon">Ambon</a> in 1512, allied himself with the ruler of Ternate and built a fortress there. From 1546 Portuguese missionary work under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/francis-xavier">Francis Xavier</a> created large Christian communities. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dutch">Dutch</a> arrived in 1599, and in 1605 the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dutch-east-india-company">Dutch East India Company</a>, allied with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sultan">Sultan</a> of Ternate, conquered Ambon and Tidore and expelled the Portuguese. It then monopolised the production and trade of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spice-trade">spices</a> by a ruthless policy, including the genocidal conquest of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nutmeg">nutmeg</a>-producing <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/banda-islands">Banda Islands</a> in 1621, the elimination of the English on Ambon in 1623 and the subordination of Ternate and Tidore in the 1650s.</p><p>The Nuku Rebellion, led by a Tidore prince, spread across much of Maluku and Papua in 1780-1810. British forces held the islands in 1796-1801 and again in 1810-1817, and uprooted many spice trees for planting elsewhere in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/british-empire">British Empire</a>. After <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/indonesian-independence">Indonesian independence</a> a Republic of South Maluku was declared in 1950 and was put down by the Indonesian army.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>Since 1999 the islands have formed two Indonesian provinces: Maluku, with its capital at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ambon">Ambon</a>, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/north-maluku">North Maluku</a>, with its capital at Sofifi on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/halmahera">Halmahera</a>. Their combined population in 2020 was 3,131,860. Over 130 languages were once spoken, and many speakers have moved to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/criollo-people">creoles</a> of Ternate and Ambon. Cloves and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nutmeg">nutmeg</a> are still grown, alongside cocoa, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/coffee-economy">coffee</a> and fruit, and fishing is a large industry.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Maluku Islands” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>On 1 January 1800 its territories, centred on Batavia and parts of Java, the <b>Moluccas</b> and scattered trading posts, passed to the Batavian Republic.</blockquote><a href="/empire/dutch-east-indies">Dutch East Indies · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>The spice monopolies enforced across the <b>Maluku Islands</b>, covering nutmeg, cloves and mace, were maintained through deliberate violence, including the Dutch conquest of the Banda Islands under Jan Pieterszoon Coen in 1621, with the near-extermination and enslavement of their population.</blockquote><a href="/empire/dutch-empire">Dutch Empire · Socioeconomy</a></li><li><blockquote>Communal conflicts in <b>Maluku</b> and Kalimantan and the Bali bombings of 2002 tested the new democracy, and the Indian Ocean tsunami of December 2004 killed well over 100,000 people in Aceh, a disaster that helped bring the Helsinki peace agreement with the Free Aceh Movement in August 2005.</blockquote><a href="/empire/indonesia">Indonesia · Into the Present</a></li><li><blockquote>Maritime trade was central to the empire's wealth, linking ports from Sumatra and Borneo to Sulawesi and <b>Maluku</b> with international networks reaching China, India and the Malay Peninsula.</blockquote><a href="/empire/majapahit-empire">Majapahit Empire · Socioeconomy</a></li><li><blockquote>Ships from China, the Ryukyu Islands, Java and the <b>Maluku Islands</b> came in on the northeast monsoon from December to January, and ships for the Indian coasts, the Red Sea and East Africa left on the southwest monsoon.</blockquote><a href="/empire/malacca-sultanate">Malacca Sultanate · Socioeconomy</a></li><li><blockquote>…met by slave raiding: Iranun and Balangingi Sama fleets based in the Sulu zone seized, by his estimates, thousands of captives a year from the Visayas, Luzon, the <b>Moluccas</b>, Sulawesi and the Malay world, many of whom were set to fishing and collecting marine produce or sold onward.</blockquote><a href="/empire/sulu-sultanate">Sulu Sultanate · Socioeconomy</a></li></ul></section>
